==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|stān}}, {{m|goh|stēn}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*stāną}}. The ''-h-'' was introduced into the spelling by analogy with {{m|de|sehen}}, in which it had become mute but was retained in spelling.

===Alternative forms===
* {{m|de|stehn}} {{qualifier|dated in formal prose, but still common informally or poetically}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃteː.ən/|lang=de}} {{a|official standard, but less common}}
* {{IPA|/ʃteːn/|lang=de}} {{a|predominant}}
* {{hyphenation|ste|hen|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-stehen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-stehen.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|eːən|eːn|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-irregular|steht|stand|gestanden|auxiliary=both|past subjunctive=[[stände]] or [[stünde]]}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[stand]] (to be upright, support oneself on the feet in an erect position)
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[be]], to [[appear]], to [[stand]] (to be placed or located somewhere)
#: {{ux|de|Das '''steht''' nicht in dem Wörterbuch.|This does not '''appear''' in the dictionary.}}
#* '''1931''', {{w|Arthur Schnitzler}}, ''[[s:de:Flucht in die Finsternis|Flucht in die Finsternis]]'', {{w|S. Fischer Verlag}}, page 36:
#*: Ein frisch gefülltes Glas Champagner '''stand''' vor ihm. Er trank es in einem Zug aus – mit Lust, fast mit Begier.
#*:: A freshly filled glass of champaign was in front of him. He emptied it in one draught – with pleasure, almost with greed.
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[stay]]; to be [[still]]

====Usage notes====
The most frequent auxiliary with ''stehen'' is {{m|de|haben}}: ''Ich habe gestanden.'' In northern and central Germany, only this form is used. In southern Germany, Austria, and Switzerland, {{m|de|sein}} is common in the vernacular and also, alternatively, in standard usage: ''Ich bin gestanden.''

